## Setting a target

Select an item in the binder and open the Inspector. Enter the number of words you want to
write in *Target*. If your project counts characters, enter a character target instead.
Set it to zero to remove the target.

Set a target for each chapter or scene you want to track. A chapter’s target is not
automatically calculated from its scenes’ targets.

## Where you stand

You can check your progress in several places. The Inspector shows it under the *Target*
field. The status bar shows it beside the live count, and the *Overview* table gives it a
*Target* column.

## What counts toward a target

Progress is measured on the manuscript itself: the prose of your
[scenes and chapters](:help-writing-model). A synopsis, a note or a paratext stays out of the
count, and so does anything in the trash.

Turning off *Include in exports* in the Inspector also removes that item from the count.
Turning it off for a chapter does not automatically exclude its scenes. Use *Apply to children*
to exclude them too.

## Words or characters

One choice covers the whole project. *New Work* asks for it up front, under *Count in*. You can
change it later in *Settings ▸ Work: <project> ▸ Structure*, under *Targets*.

Each item keeps two separate numbers: a word target and a character target.

## Sharing a target out

To divide a book’s target among its parts or chapters, open the book and click *Distribute…*.
Set a target first if the button is disabled. You can do the same for a part or chapter folder.
The preview shows the proposed targets. Under *Share out*, choose:

- *By length*, from what is already written under each;
- *By pieces*;
- *Evenly*.

Chapters and scenes with existing targets keep them; the others share what remains. Turn on
*Replace targets that are already set* to recalculate all of them.

## Planning a pace

Open a book’s *Pace* section and choose *Start planning* to set a daily writing goal.

Fill in *Word goal* and *Deadline*, then pick the weekdays you write on. Skribisto works out
what each remaining writing day needs, how many are left, and whether you are ahead of or
behind pace.

Add time off under *Holidays* to leave those days out of the plan.

Pace and milestones count in words, whatever unit the project uses.

## Milestones

A milestone marks a date along the way.

- Under *Milestones* in the planner, add one for the whole book: a word count it should reach
  by that date.
- On a part or a chapter inside a book, set a *Milestone date* in the Inspector. It reads that
  item's own word target, and follows it.

## When the project opens

A book appears in the *Where the book stands* card once its plan is active. *Do not show this
when opening* stops it; *Work ▸ Writing plan…* reopens it.
